This scenario represents a dynamic series of connected elements, which is best modeled using a **Singly Linked List** where the engine serves as the head node, and each compartment is a successive node.

### 1. Data Structure Definition

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Compartment {
    int compartment_id;
    struct Compartment* next;
};

```

### 2. Attaching a Compartment
To attach a new compartment at the end of the train:

```c
void attachCompartment(struct Compartment* engine, int id) {
    struct Compartment* newComp = (struct Compartment*)malloc(sizeof(struct Compartment));
    newComp->compartment_id = id;
    newComp->next = NULL;

    struct Compartment* temp = engine;
    while (temp->next != NULL) {
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    temp->next = newComp;
    printf("Compartment %d attached to the train.\n", id);
}

```

### 3. Detaching a Compartment
To detach a compartment given its ID:

```c
void detachCompartment(struct Compartment* engine, int id) {
    struct Compartment* temp = engine;
    struct Compartment* prev = NULL;

    while (temp != NULL && temp->compartment_id != id) {
        prev = temp;
        temp = temp->next;
    }

    if (temp == NULL) {
        printf("Compartment %d not found!\n", id);
        return;
    }

    // Unlink the node from the list
    prev->next = temp->next;
    free(temp);
    printf("Compartment %d detached from the train.\n", id);
}
```
